WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:13.280
Every evening, a dramatic show takes place
at the border between Pakistan and India.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.650
It’s an over the top display of strength
and nationalism from both sides.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.550
But while this show-down is mostly ceremonial,
the animosity between these two nuclear-armed

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.400
countries is very real.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.820
A lot of this bitter rivalry can be traced
back to when Pakistan and India were still

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.120
united under British rule. Back to this moment.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:50.520
For nearly 200 years, the people of India
lived under British rule.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.800
At the time, dominant religious groups in
the country included Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.460
The British colonized this large nation through
a policy of "divide and rule" that pitted

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:05.810
these religious groups against each other.
The strategy was that Indians would be busy

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:09.270
fighting each other — instead of British
imperial rule.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:14.180
But after decades of violence and oppression
by the British, people found a reason to come

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.940
together: a movement for independence.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:26.330
The British responded with brutality and violence.
But eventually, the movement for independence

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:32.240
led to a decision that would create a political
rift between Hindus and Muslims.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.430
Britain passed the Government of India Act
of 1935. It gave Indian provinces a new political

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:42.260
structure, while still under the authority
of the British crown.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.070
The act set up local governments with dedicated
seats for representatives from religious groups

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:53.060
including: Hindus who got "general seats",
along with Sikhs, and Muslims - or "Muhammadan".

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.720
Hindus and Muslims in particular had been
at odds at other points in British India’s

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.640
history — but the act created even more
division through assigned seats for religious groups.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:08.700
It was all part of the British divide-and-rule
strategy.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.400
In the resulting elections, a Hindu-majority
party called the Indian National Congress,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:20.440
was led by Jawaharlal Nehru. Another party,
the All-India Muslim League, was led by Muhammad Ali Jinnah.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:27.090
Nehru and Congress won over 700 legislative
seats, while Jinnah and the Muslim League

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.349
only won 106.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:34.219
The majority of Muslims voted for other, smaller
parties, rather than aligning with a national

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:35.879
political identity.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:40.550
Without majority representation in the new
governments, Jinnah worried the Muslim population

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.510
was increasingly vulnerable to oppression
by Hindu leadership.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.180
And his fears weren’t entirely unfounded.
Violence against Muslims had broken out in

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.750
some parts of the country.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.469
Jinnah expressed his concerns in a speech
to the Muslim League: “We are now, therefore,

00:02:55.469 --> 00:02:59.030
very apprehensive and can trust nobody.”

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:04.530
This was a striking turn for a man who had
once been an ambassador for Hindu-Muslim unity.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.219
Just 20 years earlier, Jinnah dismissed the
concern that “Muslims could become a minority

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:14.060
in what might become a Hindu state” as a
“bogey put before you by your enemies to

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.209
frighten you from cooperation with Hindus”

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:22.889
But by 1945, especially after the defeat in
elections, he shifted focus. Jinnah called

00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:27.999
for an independent nation of Pakistan in which
Muslims would be in the majority.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:32.069
He warned: A United India “meant slavery
for Mussulmans.”

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:36.319
“Muslims insisted on their demand for their
own separate state and complete independence

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.460
from the Hindu majority.”

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.769
This time, Muslims rallied around Jinnah’s
new focus on Pakistan and his call for “direct

00:03:44.769 --> 00:03:47.300
action,” where he asked Muslims to strike

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.540
and show unity in their demand for an independent state.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.660
But a rally in Calcutta, which was home to
both Hindus and Muslims, turned violent.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:07.520
Thousands of people&nbsp;died in what is known
as the Great Calcutta Killings of 1946.

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:22.419
In this atmosphere of civil unrest, Britain
announced their withdrawal from India.

00:04:22.419 --> 00:04:28.770
And it fell to this man, Lord Mountbatten,
the Viceroy of India, to broker a deal between

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.830
Nehru and Jinnah and to organize Britain’s
exit from India.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:39.480
It was at this moment that these men agreed
to the partition of India along religious lines

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.200
Jinnah would get Pakistan, the new nation
that he believed would

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.140
protect Muslims from oppression.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.540
Nehru, fearing more religious violence in
India, agreed to the plan.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:54.700
“He agrees to the partition of colonial
India into two separate religious states&nbsp;—

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:01.520
Pakistan with 100 million Muslims is created
as a counterpart to India with 300 million Hindus."

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.590
A British lawyer named Cyril Radcliffe, who
had never been to India was given less than

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:13.210
40 days to draw up the new borders based on
outdated maps and census data.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:17.680
Radcliffe kept the region with the Muslim
League’s political base in India, instead

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.650
of including it within Pakistan’s borders.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:26.430
And his borders also left most economic centers
like Delhi, Bombay and Calcutta within India,

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.180
leaving the future Pakistan at an economic
disadvantage.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:35.659
To make matters worse, the British &nbsp;unexpectedly
moved the date of their exit up by ten months.

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:39.659
They said it was important to get the two
countries “launched and on their way,”

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:44.870
because “the longer you keep them hanging
about, the greater the difficulties that arise.”

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:49.790
But behind the scenes, Britain had been battered
in the second world war, and no longer had

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:53.940
the resources to continue its control over
India.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:59.300
And so Britain rushed out of India, and finally
gave them independence. Which meant that this

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.470
moment —&nbsp;one that was supposed to ease
tensions between Muslims and Hindus — was

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.960
actually a botched handover of power.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:25.860
“Millions of Indians, led by Prime Minister
Nehru, celebrated the independence of this

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:30.919
great nation. Led by Muhammad Ali Jinnah,
the new dominion of Pakistan also celebrated

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.260
its independence on August 15, 1947.”

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.420
It marked the origins of India and Pakistan,
with horrific bloodshed.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.620
The partition of India led to violent riots
and mass migration along the newly created borders.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:53.280
Fearing religious persecution, many Muslims
fled to Pakistan, while Hindus and Sikhs moved

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.190
across the border to India.

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:57.770
But many never made it to their destinations.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:03.690
“In the trail of murder and arson come the
refugees. Their suffering is the new tragedy

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:08.389
of India. Carrying their few possessions,
they flee savagery and butchery that has never

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:10.760
been exceeded even in India’s stormy history.”

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.840
About 1 million people died and over 14 million
were displaced.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:22.060
Most of the bloodshed happened here in Punjab
and Bengal, where the new border cut through

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:26.360
provinces that were home to Muslims, Hindus
and Sikhs.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:31.389
And another province, Kashmir, which fell
on the border, became the center of a different

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:36.569
conflict -- a dispute over ownership that
would define the rivalry between the two nations

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:38.030
for decades to come.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:40.699
“Already the province of Kashmir is in dispute.”

00:07:40.699 --> 00:07:45.500
“India is trying to hold down by force what
she managed to take by fraud”

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.000
“The present hostilities originated with
large-scale infiltrations from armed personnel

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.330
originating from Pakistan”

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:57.060
“If they have any idea of taking a chunk
of my territory in East Pakistan, this will

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:58.060
mean war!” “

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:12.100
“Indians gunned down a plane which had no
capability of doing anything in self-defense.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.749
It’s a barbaric act.”

00:08:13.749 --> 00:08:21.130
“This is an India that will return the damage
done by terrorists with interest.”

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:36.530
The rivalry between India and Pakistan has
persisted for over 70 years. But it’s important

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:41.780
to remember where it all began -- a British
strategy that was designed to create a religious

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:45.100
divide between people of one nation.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.840
So this was an incredibly complex story for us to try and cover in 9 minutes.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:58.100
The animosity between Hindus and Muslims actually predates the British empire.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:03.400
But for this story, we really wanted to cover how religious divisions became politically defined

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:04.260
under British rule.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.320
And also in this video, I mention Kashmir as being a major point of conflict

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.040
that came out of the partition of India

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:15.080
We didn't go very in-depth into it in this video at all, but my colleague Sam Ellis made a great episode

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:19.760
of Vox Atlas that dives deep into Kashmir specifically, so be sure to check that out

